== LadybugDB (liblbug) ==

Vulngraph's embedded in-memory graph engine is LadybugDB
(https://github.com/LadybugDB/ladybug), the community-maintained successor of
Kùzu (https://github.com/kuzudb/kuzu, archived), used unmodified
as the prebuilt shared library of a pinned release (scripts/fetch-ladybug.sh,
docs/adr/095-upstream-ladybugdb.md).
LadybugDB's license carries the copyright notices of both Kùzu Inc. and
Ladybug Memory Inc.
This repository contains no LadybugDB source: only its C header
(internal/graphdb/ladybug/lbug.h) and its MIT license (internal/graphdb/LICENSE).
The Go binding in internal/graphdb/ladybug started from the upstream LadybugDB
Go binding and is MIT-licensed under the same upstream copyright notices
(internal/graphdb/ladybug/LICENSE).

The library bundles LadybugDB's own third-party C++ dependencies (DuckDB-derived
code, {fmt}, ANTLR and the openCypher grammar, zstd, mbedtls, re2, utf8proc
and others), each under its own license. They are listed in the upstream
repository at the pinned tag:
https://github.com/LadybugDB/ladybug/tree/v0.22.3/third_party. The library links
OpenSSL 3 dynamically; it is provided by the operating system (Debian libssl3t64),
not shipped by this project.

== License texts in the container image ==

The image ships the license texts of everything it distributes under
/usr/share/doc/graph/, next to this NOTICE, LICENSE and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md.
They are collected at build time (Dockerfile, scripts/collect-licenses.sh,
scripts/fetch-ladybug.sh --licenses-dir), and the build fails if a linked
component has none:

  licenses/go/<module path>/       every Go module linked into /graph
  licenses/go-stdlib-LICENSE       the Go standard library and runtime
  licenses/npm/<package>/          every npm package that can reach the
                                   embedded web UI (production dependencies)
  licenses/ladybug/LICENSE         LadybugDB (liblbug)
  licenses/ladybug/third_party/    the C++ components bundled in liblbug, from
                                   the source archive of the pinned tag; for
                                   single-header components and adapted files
                                   that carry their notice inline, the notice
                                   is extracted into <file>.NOTICE; where that
                                   notice is not the full licence text
                                   (cpp-httplib, fast_float, and the DuckDB and
                                   robin-hood-hashing code in checksum.cpp), the
                                   upstream LICENSE at the bundled version is
                                   shipped beside it; the Cypher parser in
                                   third_party/antlr4_cypher/ ships the
                                   openCypher grammar's Apache-2.0 licence
                                   (openCypher-LICENSE) and its NOTICE (openCypher-NOTICE)

The MPL-2.0 modules listed under "Go dependencies" are shipped unmodified;
their source is available from the upstream locations given there.

== Vulnerability data sources ==

Vulngraph fetches vulnerability data at runtime from the sources below and does
not redistribute it, except for the small trimmed excerpts kept as test
fixtures (internal/intel/osv/testdata, internal/intel/updateinfo/testdata).
Each source's own terms apply to its data.

  OSV.dev (https://osv.dev), Google — aggregated advisories, including:
    Red Hat security data (https://security.access.redhat.com/data/),
    Copyright Red Hat, Inc., licensed under CC BY 4.0;
    SUSE security advisories (https://ftp.suse.com/pub/projects/security/),
    Copyright SUSE LLC.
  Amazon Linux Security Center advisories (ALAS, https://alas.aws.amazon.com),
    read from the Amazon Linux repositories' updateinfo metadata
    (cdn.amazonlinux.com). Copyright Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates.
  Oracle Linux errata (ELSA, https://linux.oracle.com/errata/), read from the
    Oracle Linux yum server's updateinfo metadata (yum.oracle.com).
    Copyright Oracle and/or its affiliates.
  CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog and FIRST.org EPSS scores.

